          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Poetic Style and Major Works</strong><br>\nShelley's poetic style is characterized by its lyrical beauty, political engagement, and idealistic passion. His major works, including \"Ozymandias,\" \"To a Skylark,\" and \"Prometheus Unbound,\" display his mastery of language and deep philosophical inquiry into human condition and natural world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Philosophy and Influence</strong><br>\nShelley was a proponent of the Romantic movement's ideals, emphasizing emotion and the individual's connection with nature. He was also a political radical, advocating for nonviolent resistance and social reform, which he expressed through his poetry and essays.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Marriage and Exile</strong><br>\nIn 1816, Shelley remarried Mary Wollstonecraft Godwin, who was the daughter of feminist writer Mary Wollstonecraft and political philosopher William Godwin. They left England and moved throughout Europe, where Shelley continued to write and engage with other literary figures.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
