          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Tragic Losses</strong><br>\nShelley faced personal tragedies with the death of his children and the complex difficulties in his marital life, which had a profound effect on his writing. He often contemplated themes of loss, redemption, and the cruelty of time and fate within his poetry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Final Years and Masterpieces</strong><br>\nDuring the last years of his life, Shelley produced some of his finest work. His poetic output during this period was prolific and innovative, reflecting his maturation as a poet and thinker. He continued to explore radical ideas and push the boundaries of the poetic form.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shelley's Untimely Death</strong><br>\nShelley died in a boating accident in Italy in 1822 at the age of 29. His premature death cemented his reputation as a tragic figure of the Romantic era. The power and depth of his work have made him one of the most revered and studied poets in English literature, and his ideas continue to resonate with readers and thinkers today.</p>\n"
              },
